<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Hasanlu</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 BCE – 800 BCE (approx.) · capital Hasanlu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A fortified Iron Age town south of Lake Urmia, frozen in time by its violent destruction around 800 BCE, which preserved its famous gold bowl.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hasanlu Tepe had been occupied since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, but its best-known phase, Hasanlu IV, dates from about 1100 to 800 BCE, when a fortified citadel with columned halls dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solduz">Solduz</a> valley. Some scholars identify it with Gilzanu or a centre of the people later known as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mannaea">Mannaea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The citadel held temples or palace halls with wooden pillars, storerooms of horse gear, weapons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a>, and evidence of contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>. The Hasanlu gold bowl, decorated with mythological scenes, is its most famous find. Its rulers, language and political structure are unknown, since no local texts survive; the community seems to have been an elite of horse-riding warriors controlling the valley.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 800 BCE the citadel was stormed and burnt, probably by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu</a> expanding south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> excavations under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-h-dyson-jr">Robert Dyson</a> (1956 to 1977) found hundreds of bodies, including the pair known as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanlu-lovers">Hasanlu Lovers</a>'.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hasanlu.jpg" alt="Hasanlu" width="320" height="209"><figcaption>Image: Amin gholizad at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naghade-hasanloo2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 BCE – 800 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hasanlu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hasanlu Tepe, Gilzanu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-950&amp;focus=hasanlu" aria-label="Hasanlu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5819 845 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hasanlu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6139 1101L6077 1051"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6077 1051h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6139 1101h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6139 1101h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-950&amp;focus=hasanlu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

